Below is a minimal, working example of how to implement and expose a custom “REST-like” X++ service in Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations (D365FO). In D365FO, “custom services” are invoked over HTTP and exchange JSON, but calls are always POST and aren’t strictly REST. The steps and code below follow best practices for data contracts, service methods, and service exposure.

What you’ll build
- A request contract
- A response DTO
- A response contract that can return a list
- A service class with two operations
- How to expose it via a Service and Service Group
- How to call it over HTTP with JSON

1) Create data contracts (X++)

a) Simple DTO for a customer
[DataContractAttribute]
class DemoCustCustomerDto
{
    Str accountNum;
    Str name;
    Str currency;

    [DataMemberAttribute('AccountNum')]
    public str parmAccountNum(str _value = accountNum)
    {
        accountNum = _value;
        return accountNum;
    }

    [DataMemberAttribute('Name')]
    public str parmName(str _value = name)
    {
        name = _value;
        return name;
    }

    [DataMemberAttribute('Currency')]
    public str parmCurrency(str _value = currency)
    {
        currency = _value;
        return currency;
    }
}

b) Request contract
[DataContractAttribute]
class DemoCustRequest
{
    Str accountNum;
    Str nameLike;

    [DataMemberAttribute('AccountNum')]
    public str parmAccountNum(str _value = accountNum)
    {
        accountNum = _value;
        return accountNum;
    }

    [DataMemberAttribute('NameLike')]
    public str parmNameLike(str _value = nameLike)
    {
        nameLike = _value;
        return nameLike;
    }
}

c) Response contract returning a single DTO
[DataContractAttribute]
class DemoCustSingleResponse
{
    DemoCustCustomerDto customer;
    boolean success;
    str message;

    [DataMemberAttribute('Customer')]
    public DemoCustCustomerDto parmCustomer(DemoCustCustomerDto _value = customer)
    {
        customer = _value;
        return customer;
    }

    [DataMemberAttribute('Success')]
    public boolean parmSuccess(boolean _value = success)
    {
        success = _value;
        return success;
    }

    [DataMemberAttribute('Message')]
    public str parmMessage(str _value = message)
    {
        message = _value;
        return message;
    }
}

d) Response contract returning a list of DTOs
[DataContractAttribute]
class DemoCustListResponse
{
    List customers; // List of DemoCustCustomerDto
    boolean success;
    str message;

    public void new()
    {
        customers = new List(Types::Class);
    }

    [DataMemberAttribute('Customers'),
     AifCollectionTypeAttribute('Customers', Types::Class, classStr(DemoCustCustomerDto))]
    public List parmCustomers(List _value = customers)
    {
        customers = _value;
        return customers;
    }

    [DataMemberAttribute('Success')]
    public boolean parmSuccess(boolean _value = success)
    {
        success = _value;
        return success;
    }

    [DataMemberAttribute('Message')]
    public str parmMessage(str _value = message)
    {
        message = _value;
        return message;
    }
}

2) Create the service class (X++)

[SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]
public class DemoCustomerService
{
    // Returns one customer by AccountNum
    public static DemoCustSingleResponse getCustomerByAccount(DemoCustRequest _request)
    {
        DemoCustSingleResponse response = new DemoCustSingleResponse();
        DemoCustCustomerDto dto;
        CustTable cust;

        try
        {
            if (!_request || !_request.parmAccountNum())
            {
                response.parmSuccess(false);
                response.parmMessage("AccountNum is required.");
                return response;
            }

            cust = CustTable::find(_request.parmAccountNum(), true);
            if (!cust.RecId)
            {
                response.parmSuccess(false);
                response.parmMessage(strFmt("Customer %1 not found.", _request.parmAccountNum()));
                return response;
            }

            dto = new DemoCustCustomerDto();
            dto.parmAccountNum(cust.AccountNum);
            dto.parmName(cust.name());
            dto.parmCurrency(cust.Currency);

            response.parmCustomer(dto);
            response.parmSuccess(true);
            response.parmMessage("OK");
        }
        catch (Exception::Error)
        {
            response.parmSuccess(false);
            response.parmMessage(AifUtil::getClrErrorMessage());
        }

        return response;
    }

    // Returns a list of customers whose name contains NameLike
    public static DemoCustListResponse findCustomers(DemoCustRequest _request)
    {
        DemoCustListResponse response = new DemoCustListResponse();
        List list = new List(Types::Class);
        DemoCustCustomerDto dto;
        CustTable cust;

        try
        {
            if (!_request || !_request.parmNameLike())
            {
                response.parmSuccess(false);
                response.parmMessage("NameLike is required.");
                return response;
            }

            while select AccountNum, Party, Currency from cust
                where cust.name() like '*' + _request.parmNameLike() + '*'
            {
                dto = new DemoCustCustomerDto();
                dto.parmAccountNum(cust.AccountNum);
                dto.parmName(cust.name());
                dto.parmCurrency(cust.Currency);
                list.addEnd(dto);
            }

            response.parmCustomers(list);
            response.parmSuccess(true);
            response.parmMessage("OK");
        }
        catch (Exception::Error)
        {
            response.parmSuccess(false);
            response.parmMessage(AifUtil::getClrErrorMessage());
        }

        return response;
    }
}

3) Expose the service
- In Visual Studio (D365FO model):
  1) Create a Service object named DemoCustomerService, set its class to DemoCustomerService, and add operations for:
     - getCustomerByAccount
     - findCustomers
  2) Create a Service Group named DemoDemoServices, add your DemoCustomerService to it, set Auto deploy to Yes, then build and deploy the service group (right-click Deploy Service Group if needed).
  3) Security: Create a Privilege that grants access to the service operations (Object type: ServiceOperation), add it to a Duty/Role, and assign the role to your app registration user or interactive user.

4) Call the service over HTTP (JSON)
- All custom service calls use POST and JSON.
- URL format:
  https://{your-environment}.cloudax.dynamics.com/api/services/{ServiceGroupName}/{ServiceName}/{OperationName}

Examples

a) Get a single customer
- URL:
  https://{host}/api/services/DemoDemoServices/DemoCustomerService/getCustomerByAccount
- Method: POST
- Headers:
  - Authorization: Bearer {AAD_access_token}
  - Content-Type: application/json
- Body:
  {
    "request": {
      "AccountNum": "US-001"
    }
  }
- Notes:
  - The JSON root properties must match your X++ parameter names and DataMember names.
  - Because our method signature is (DemoCustRequest request), the JSON has a “request” node.
- Sample response:
  {
    "Customer": {
      "AccountNum": "US-001",
      "Name": "Contoso Retail",
      "Currency": "USD"
    },
    "Success": true,
    "Message": "OK"
  }

b) Find customers by name substring
- URL:
  https://{host}/api/services/DemoDemoServices/DemoCustomerService/findCustomers
- Body:
  {
    "request": {
      "NameLike": "Contoso"
    }
  }
- Sample response:
  {
    "Customers": [
      { "AccountNum": "US-001", "Name": "Contoso Retail", "Currency": "USD" },
      { "AccountNum": "US-002", "Name": "Contoso Wholesale", "Currency": "USD" }
    ],
    "Success": true,
    "Message": "OK"
  }

5) OAuth token acquisition (Abridged)
- Acquire an AAD token for the D365FO resource (app ID URL) using your Azure AD app registration.
- Example (client credentials flow):
  POST https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenantId}/oauth2/token
  grant_type=client_credentials
  client_id={appId}
  client_secret={secret}
  resource={D365FO_appId_URI}   (often https://{host})

6) Notes and tips
- Parameter naming: The JSON payload must match the parameter name in your X++ method signature. Use simple names like “request” to keep JSON clean.
- Collections: When returning lists of classes in data contracts, always add AifCollectionTypeAttribute to the parm method to declare element type.
- Exceptions: Catch and return user-friendly messages. Uncaught exceptions will be rendered as HTTP 500.
- Verb semantics: Custom services are always POST. If you need real REST with GET/PUT/PATCH, prefer the OData endpoint for entities or build an Azure Function/Proxy.
- AX 2012: If you’re on AX 2012, custom services are exposed via AIF/WCF. The pattern is similar (service class + service + service group + inbound port). JSON “REST” support is limited; many implementations use SOAP or a custom IIS/WCF wrapper. If you need AX 2012 instructions, say which CU/version and I’ll tailor the steps.

This template should compile and run in D365FO and is easy to extend to create or update records. If you want a “create customer” operation, I can share a safe version that handles the party/addresses requirements correctly.

